#!/usr/bin/env python3
r"""NYXUS Suxyn — the tape: keep the song you are hearing.       TRK-3817

ARM it, and every song that plays through from its start is kept into your
own library — the audio, the artist, the title, the album, the cover art and
the synced words, as files you own and can play with the power off.

    nyxus-tape --arm          start collecting
    nyxus-tape --disarm       stop
    nyxus-tape --resume       re-honour a standing `armed: true` at session
                              start. Started by an exec-once; starts NOTHING
                              if he had it off. See resume()'s docstring for
                              the measurement that opened it     TRK-3959
    nyxus-tape --toggle       the bar's right-click entry and Settings both
                              call this; there is one state, two surfaces
    nyxus-tape --status       JSON: armed, what is on tape, what was kept
    nyxus-tape --probe        JSON: is this image able to do it at all

── ⚠ THE STATE IS A FACT, AND THESE ARE THE FIELDS THAT MAKE IT ONE ───────
`--status` prints the state file CORRECTED AGAINST /proc, never the file as
written. The three fields a surface must not ignore:  TRK-3829

    "beat":    unix seconds, re-stamped every 10 s by the running daemon.
               A surface that reads ~/.config/nyxus/tape.json directly (the
               bar does) has no pid to check, so it treats a heartbeat older
               than 45 s as OFF. This is what makes a SIGKILLed daemon decay
               to "off" on his bar instead of lying there lit for ever.
    "stale":   true when the FILE said armed and reality disagreed. Always
               present, true or false -- it used to be written only on the
               failing branch, so a reader could not tell "fresh" from "this
               build does not say".
    "daemons": how many tape daemons are actually running. It is 1 when
               armed and 0 when not. TWO IS THE DEFECT of 2026-09-07 and a
               surface should be able to see it rather than infer it.

Exit codes: 0 done · 4 tools missing · 5 the daemon did not come up ·
6 --disarm could not stop it (the state is deliberately LEFT ARMED, because
a control that cannot stop the tape must not draw itself as off).

── ⚠ THIS RECORDS YOUR OWN OUTPUT. IT IS NOT A DOWNLOADER. ────────────────
Written down because the next lane WILL be tempted to "improve" it, and the
improvement would be a different feature with a different meaning.

This reads the PulseAudio monitor of the sink the player is already feeding
— the same signal your speakers get, on your machine, that you are already
listening to. It is taping the radio. It never contacts a music service, it
holds no session, it sends no identifiers, and it cannot fetch anything you
are not currently playing: a four-minute song takes four minutes, because
there is nothing here that goes faster than sound.

⛔ DO NOT replace this with yt-dlp or any stream-ripper. That is not what was
asked for ("keep the song we hear"), it is a materially different act, and it
would break the one property that makes this honest: **you can only keep what
you are actually listening to.**

── ⚠ ARM/DISARM IS THE WHOLE DESIGN, AND IT IS THE OWNER'S ────────────────
The obvious shape — "press KEEP on the song you like" — cannot work, because
by the time you press it the song is half over. The expensive answer is a
permanent rolling buffer, and this build's stated principle is that idle cost
is the point.

The owner's own instruction removed the problem: *"i want a button to turn it
on or off incase i need to switch through songs that way i dont record
2seconds then i skips."* So the model is not "keep this one", it is
**"I am collecting now"**:

  DISARMED (the default)  Nothing runs. No daemon, no `parec`, no buffer, no
                          disk, no CPU, no audio device held. Idle cost is
                          not "small", it is **zero**.
  ARMED                   Every qualifying track that plays FROM ITS START is
                          taped whole, because arming happened before the
                          track began.

Consequence, deliberate and stated: **arming in the middle of a song does not
keep that song.** The tape starts at the next track boundary. A half-track is
not a library file, and a feature that silently produced them is the exact
pile of two-second fragments the owner asked for a button to prevent. The
surfaces say "starts with the next song" rather than pretending otherwise.

No rolling buffer was built. The only buffer here is PREROLL_SEC (0.6 s), and
it exists for a different reason — see THE HEAD, below.

── ⚠ COMMERCIALS. THE OWNER ASKED, AND HE WAS RIGHT TO. ───────────────────
"one thing real fast what about if it plays comericals". A recorder that
opens a stream and writes until the track ends splices an advert into the
middle of a saved song, and you find out months later while listening to it.

MEASURED ON THE OWNER'S OWN SESSION, 2026-09-07 01:20, YouTube Music free
tier in Chromium — not theorised:

    01:20:16  Playing | South River Technologies | WebDrive | …  len 36.301s
    01:20:17  <no player at all, for one poll>
    01:20:17  Playing | Dove US | Dove Body Wash | 15s | …       len 15.021s
    01:20:32  Stopped | (artist, title, album ALL EMPTY)         len 0
    01:20:33  Playing | TheHipHopCanal | Lil Wayne - Run This Town  len 164.961s

Four things follow, and two of them kill the obvious implementation:

1. **`xesam:artist`, `xesam:title` and `mpris:length` DO change for an
   advert.** MPRIS is a reliable ad signal here. Good.
2. ⚠ **`mpris:trackid` DOES NOT CHANGE. EVER.** All five rows above carry the
   identical `…/Track5279E7E6D748D91A0EC5A4AF0CC1AE43` — two different adverts
   AND two different songs. Chromium publishes one static trackid for the
   whole tab. **Keying the recorder on trackid, which is the first thing
   anybody would write, would have spliced every advert into every file and
   looked correct while doing it.** Never trackid.
   ⚠ Identity is `xesam:artist` + `xesam:title`, with `mpris:length` checked
   separately to a tolerance — NOT as part of the key. An earlier revision
   made length part of the key and it was one millisecond from deleting
   finished songs at random; `Now.ident` carries the measurement.
3. ⚠ **`xesam:album` being empty is NOT an ad signal**, however plausible it
   looks. It is empty for the adverts *and* empty for "TheHipHopCanal | Lil
   Wayne - Run This Town", a perfectly real track played from a YouTube
   video. A heuristic on album would have thrown away real songs.
4. There is a transient state at every boundary where the player briefly
   vanishes or reports `Stopped` with every field blank. That is NOT an
   error and must not be treated as one — it is simply "not my track", which
   closes the gate.

── ⚠ THE AD BREAK, OBSERVED END TO END WITH ITS OUTCOME ───────────────────
The 01:20 rows above are transitions. This one is the whole event WITH what
the tape actually did, captured 2026-09-07 01:44 on his session while he was
listening, armed, unprompted — the proof that the rule below works:

    01:43:47  Playing | City Girls | Good Love | RAW | 232.801s   ← taping
    01:44:33  Stopped | City Girls | Good Love …  trackid → …/NoTrack
    01:44:34  Playing | NEMLUVIO… | Scratch Resistance Pool Out 30 | 30.061s
    01:45:04  Playing | Pantene   | Pantene-KOL-ENG-SunkissGlow…   | 36.281s
    01:45:41  Playing | Imperium Music Group | Pretty Problems | 131.000s

  → `Good Love.flac` was written at 01:44, at the song's end and BEFORE the
    first advert. Neither advert opened a tape (30.1 s and 36.3 s are under
    the 60 s floor) and neither reached a file. The next song opened a clean
    tape of its own. `silencedetect` over the finished file finds exactly one
    span above -50 dB for 1 s — at 229.4–233.0 s, the track's own outro tail,
    contiguous with the end. **No gap anywhere in the body: no splice, no
    dropout, no doubled audio at the join.**

⚠ TWO THINGS THIS RUN CORRECTED, both of which look like nothing in a log:

a) **The adverts carry a real `xesam:artist` AND `xesam:title`** ("Pantene").
   So `eligible()`'s requirement that a track be *named* rejects nothing —
   MIN_TRACK_SEC is doing ALL of the work of keeping adverts out. That is why
   the floor is a setting and why this file refuses to imply more.

b) **MPRIS fields arrive ONE AT A TIME, not as one atomic update.** At
   01:45:41 the same track was published four times in a second, gaining
   artist, then album, then `Playing` — the first row had an EMPTY artist.
   Opening a tape on a partial row would file a real song under
   "Unknown artist". Nothing here does, but only because `eligible()` also
   requires `Playing`, which is the LAST field to land. That is load-bearing
   and was previously accidental; do not relax it.

**THE RULE: audio is written ONLY while MPRIS still reports the same track
the tape was opened on.** The moment it deviates — advert, boundary, blank,
player gone — the gate closes and bytes are dropped on the floor. When the
triple comes back, the gate reopens and writing resumes into the same tape.
No new dependency, no level heuristic.

⚠ A LEVEL/SILENCE HEURISTIC WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED. It would eventually
cut a quiet passage out of a real song, which is a worse and much less
visible failure than an advert in a file.

⚠ WHAT THIS DOES NOT GUARANTEE. Adverts do not open a tape of their own
because of MIN_TRACK_SEC (60 s) — the two measured adverts were 36.3 s and
15.0 s. An advert longer than that floor, carrying a non-empty artist and
title, WOULD be eligible to open a tape. There is no field in what Chromium
publishes that separates a long advert from a short song; `mpris:trackid` is
constant, there is no `xesam:url`, and `CanSeek`/`CanGoNext`/`CanPause` were
all true during playback. So the floor is the honest bound, it is a setting,
and this docstring says so rather than the feature quietly pretending.

── ⚠ WHICH MONITOR. THERE ARE TWO AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME SIGNAL. ───────
The owner runs EasyEffects, so his machine carries two RUNNING monitors:

    easyeffects_sink.monitor                     float32le 2ch 48000Hz
    alsa_output.pci-…analog-stereo.monitor       s32le     2ch 48000Hz

MEASURED: Chromium's sink-input is on sink 34 = `easyeffects_sink`. So the
easyeffects monitor carries **the music, pre-effects**, and the hardware
monitor carries **everything the machine plays, post-effects**.

DEFAULT `tape_source = "player"` — the monitor of whatever sink the player is
actually feeding, RESOLVED AT RUNTIME rather than hardcoded. Three reasons,
in order of weight:

1. **Nothing else is on it.** The hardware sink is where notifications, event
   sounds and every other app land. Recording there bakes a notification blip
   permanently into a library file, and he finds out on playback.
2. **EasyEffects is a listening preference, not part of the song.** Baking his
   EQ into the file is irreversible, and it gets applied a SECOND time when he
   plays the file back through the same chain. Doubled EQ.
3. **It follows him.** Resolving the player's sink means moving Chromium's
   output moves the tape with it, with nothing to reconfigure.

`tape_source = "output"` records the default sink's monitor instead — literally
what came out of the speakers, effects and all — for anyone who wants that.
Both are offered because "the song we hear" is genuinely ambiguous once an
effects chain is in the path, and guessing silently is worse than asking.

⚠ READING A MONITOR IS PASSIVE. `parec` on a `.monitor` source is a reader;
it does not take the device, does not change the sink's format, and cannot
pause, seek or interrupt the player. Proven, not assumed — see
docs/proof/tape-0907/.

── ⚠ THE HEAD, AND WHY THE FILE IS TRIMMED FROM THE FRONT ─────────────────
MPRIS announces the new track slightly BEFORE its audio reaches the monitor
(the sink has tens of ms of buffering), so opening the gate on the boundary
event captures a little of the previous track's tail rather than losing this
one's head — the safe direction. PREROLL_SEC guarantees it.

The END boundary is the trustworthy one, so the finished file is aligned to
it: take the LAST `mpris:length` seconds of what was captured. That drops the
pre-roll and the previous tail exactly, with no guesswork about latency.

── ⚠ A SKIPPED TRACK IS NEVER SAVED ───────────────────────────────────────
A tape is written to the library only if its gated audio reaches
COMPLETE_FRACTION (0.95) of the track's own `mpris:length`. Skip at two
seconds and the tape is deleted, which is the entire point of the button.
Disarming mid-track deletes the tape in progress; it never leaves a stub.

── where the scratch lives, and what it costs while ARMED ─────────────────
Scratch PCM goes in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (tmpfs — RAM, not disk), so a song that
is never kept costs **zero disk writes and zero disk wear**. s16le/48k/stereo
is 187.5 KB/s, so a 4-minute song holds about 45 MB of RAM while it plays and
frees it at the boundary. MAX_TRACK_SEC caps that: past 15 minutes it is a DJ
set or a livestream, not a song, and the tape is abandoned.

── format, and why lossless is not obviously right ────────────────────────
⚠ The source is ALREADY LOSSY (YouTube Music streams compressed audio). FLAC
therefore preserves lossy audio at roughly six times the size — it buys no
fidelity over the stream. What it does buy is the avoidance of a SECOND
generation of loss, and that matters here precisely because **this capture is
unrepeatable**: it happened in real time, once.

Default `tape_format = "flac"`, because FLAC → MP3 is always available later
and MP3 → FLAC never undoes anything. `"mp3"` (320k, ID3v2.3 + APIC) is
offered for size. Both carry tags and embedded cover art; both are read by
the Media app, which takes tags from GStreamer's Discoverer.

⚠ NO mutagen. It is not installed on the owner's machine and adding a Python
dependency to ship a tag is the wrong trade when `ffmpeg` — already present,
already a package — writes Vorbis comments, ID3v2 and an embedded picture in
one invocation. Verified on his ffmpeg 9.0.1 for both containers.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import unicodedata
from pathlib import Path

# ── the constants the docstring argues for ────────────────────────────────
RATE = 48000
CHANNELS = 2
SAMPLE_BYTES = 2                     # s16le
BYTES_PER_SEC = RATE * CHANNELS * SAMPLE_BYTES      # 192000
CHUNK = 16384                        # ~85 ms; small enough that a gate edge
                                     # lands within a tenth of a second

PREROLL_SEC = 0.6                    # see THE HEAD
COMPLETE_FRACTION = 0.95             # see A SKIPPED TRACK IS NEVER SAVED
MAX_TRACK_SEC = 900                  # 15 min: past this it is not a song
HOLD_SEC = 240                       # how long a paused tape waits for its
                                     # track to come back from an advert
DEFAULT_MIN_TRACK_SEC = 60           # the honest advert floor
BEAT_SEC = 10                        # how often the daemon re-stamps `beat`
STALE_BEAT_SEC = 45                  # a heartbeat older than this is not a
                                     # running tape. See THE HEARTBEAT.
STOP_GRACE_SEC = 5.0                 # SIGTERM, then SIGKILL. See stop_daemons()
LENGTH_TOL_SEC = 2.0                 # see Now.ident: mpris:length re-publishes
                                     # with small variations DURING a track,
                                     # measured at 99 ms. Exact equality on it
                                     # silently deletes finished songs.

HOME = Path.home()
CONF_DIR = HOME / ".config" / "nyxus"
SETTINGS = CONF_DIR / "settings.json"
STATE = CONF_DIR / "tape.json"
KEEPS_DIR = HOME / "Music" / "NYXUS Keeps"

# ⚠ THE INDEX IS A CONTRACT WITH THE MEDIA APP, NOT A CACHE. TRK-3822.
# Written here, read there, specified in docs/CONTRACT_KEPT_LIBRARY.md.
# It sits beside Media's own `library.json` in the same XDG data directory
# on purpose: it is the same kind of object, it is regenerable, and it must
# never live inside ~/Music, where it would show up as a file in his library.
#
# ⚠ IT IS NOT THE SOURCE OF TRUTH. The FILES are. The index records what the
# tape cannot re-derive from a file later -- WHEN a song was kept, and which
# monitor it came off -- plus the tags, so a library view can group and sort
# without decoding every FLAC. A missing or stale index must degrade to a
# directory walk, never to an empty library: see the contract doc.
INDEX = Path(os.environ.get("XDG_DATA_HOME") or (HOME / ".local" / "share")) \
    / "nyxus" / "media" / "keeps.json"

# ══ THE COVER LEDGER ══════════════════════════════════════════════ TRK-3859
#
# ⚠ WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, BECAUSE THE BRIEF HAD IT ONE STEP OFF. The brief
# said this recorder "embeds whatever MPRIS exposed" and should be changed to
# read `mpris:artUrl` the way the bar's popup does. It ALREADY read artUrl,
# and already copied the bytes at tape-open rather than holding the path --
# the comment in `Tape.__init__` says why. So `artUrl` was never the missing
# piece; TWO other things were, and they are what changed:
#
#   1 · WHEN. The URL is sampled once, at the instant the tape opens, which
#       is the instant the metadata CHANGED. Chromium publishes a track's
#       fields in stages: the artist and title land first and `artUrl` can
#       still be pointing at the previous song's temp file, or at the
#       channel avatar YouTube shows before the album art resolves. One
#       sample at the earliest possible moment is the worst moment to take
#       it. `Tape.settle_art()` now re-copies while the metadata for THIS
#       track keeps arriving, for ART_SETTLE_SEC, so the value that gets
#       embedded is the settled one rather than the first one.
#
#   2 · WHETHER. Nothing checked that the image was a cover at all. Measured
#       on the owner's machine: 92 kept FLACs carrying essentially TWO
#       distinct images -- a channel avatar reading "JOSEPH PLAYLIST" and the
#       Chromium logo. That is a CONTENT defect; every tile in Media looked
#       identical because every file genuinely held the same picture.
#
# The test is `nyxus_media.py`'s own, moved upstream to where the damage is
# done: AN IMAGE THAT TURNS UP UNDER MORE THAN ONE ALBUM IS NOT A COVER
# (commit 3ff54348, `Library._reject_source_icons`). Here that is one lookup
# -- the sha1 of the bytes against a ledger of digest -> album -- and the
# verdict is remembered, so a source icon is caught once and skipped for
# ever after.
#
# ⚠ A WRONG COVER IS WORSE THAN NONE. When the verdict is "icon" the art is
# simply not embedded and Media draws its honest NO COVER plate.
#
# ⚠ AND THE FIRST SIGHTING CANNOT BE JUDGED. A digest with no history is
# embedded, because one observation cannot distinguish a cover from an icon.
# `--covers-scan` exists for exactly that: it reads the files already kept
# and seeds the ledger from them, so the guard is armed by history instead of
# waiting to accumulate it.
COVERS = INDEX.parent / "cover-ledger.json"
COVER_LEDGER_MAX = 5000              # digests kept; oldest dropped past this
COVER_ALBUMS_KEPT = 64               # album keys remembered per image

# ── ⚠ TWO ALBUMS WAS NOT ENOUGH EVIDENCE. THREE IS.        TRK-3960
# The original bound was "more than one album", which is the right IDEA and
# the wrong THRESHOLD, because the album key is derived from tags a browser
# publishes and those are not clean. MEASURED on his 315 kept songs, the
# distinct-album counts split with nothing anywhere near the middle:
#
#     the Chromium logo        104 distinct albums   a source icon
#     a channel avatar          16 distinct albums   a source icon
#     every real cover           1 distinct album    a cover
#     the four false convictions 1 distinct album    (2 under the old key)
#
# There is a gap of a hundred albums between the two populations and the
# rule was drawing its line at two. Every wrong verdict on his machine was a
# 2, and no correct verdict was below 16. Three costs nothing real and puts
# the line inside the gap instead of on top of the noise.
#
# ⚠ THE COST OF THE TWO DIRECTIONS IS NOT SYMMETRIC, which is why the line
# goes here and not lower. Missing an icon embeds one wrong picture in one
# file. Convicting a cover destroys a real one for every song of that album
# from then on, and the file is written once -- `--covers-scan` reads what
# was embedded, it cannot put back what never was.
COVER_ICON_ALBUMS = 3
ART_SETTLE_SEC = 25.0                # see 1 above


def runtime_dir() -> Path:
    """tmpfs if we have one, so an unkept song costs no disk wear at all."""
    rd = os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR")
    base = Path(rd) if rd and os.path.isdir(rd) else Path("/tmp")
    return base / "nyxus" / "tape"


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  settings + state
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

def read_json(path: Path) -> dict:
    """Never raises. A missing or corrupt file is 'no opinion', not an error;
    this process must never put a traceback in front of the owner."""
    try:
        d = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        return d if isinstance(d, dict) else {}
    except Exception:
        return {}


def write_json(path: Path, doc: dict) -> None:
    """Atomic, because the shell has a FileView watching this path and a
    half-written file would be parsed as garbage exactly once per write."""
    try:
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        tmp = path.with_suffix(path.suffix + ".tmp")
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps(doc, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2),
                       encoding="utf-8")
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except Exception:
        pass


def index_add(dest: Path, lrc: Path | None, now: Now, fmt: str,
              source: str, art: bool) -> None:
    """Append one kept song to the index. See docs/CONTRACT_KEPT_LIBRARY.md.

    ⚠ REBUILT BY PATH, NOT APPENDED BLINDLY. An entry whose file no longer
    exists is dropped on every write, so trashing a song from the Media app
    (which uses Gio trash, and cannot be expected to know about this file)
    cannot leave a ghost row in a library view for ever. The index converges
    on the directory instead of drifting away from it.
    """
    try:
        doc = read_json(INDEX)
        rows = [r for r in (doc.get("tracks") or [])
                if isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("path")
                and r.get("path") != str(dest)
                and os.path.exists(r["path"])]
        rows.append({
            "path": str(dest),
            "lrc": str(lrc) if lrc and lrc.exists() else None,
            "artist": now.artist,
            "album": now.album,
            "title": now.title,
            # The track's own length as the PLAYER reported it. The file's
            # decoded duration can differ by a chunk boundary; a library view
            # that wants the exact figure should read the file.
            "duration": round(now.length, 3),
            "kept_at": int(time.time()),
            "format": fmt,
            "source": source,
            "art": bool(art),
        })
        rows.sort(key=lambda r: r.get("kept_at", 0))
        INDEX.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        tmp = INDEX.with_suffix(".json.tmp")
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps({"v": 1, "dir": str(KEEPS_DIR),
                                   "tracks": rows},
                                  ensure_ascii=False, indent=2),
                       encoding="utf-8")
        os.replace(tmp, INDEX)
    except Exception:
        # ⚠ The index is a convenience. Failing to write it must never cost
        # the owner the song, which is already safely on disk by this point.
        pass


def settings() -> dict:
    return read_json(SETTINGS)


def opt_format() -> str:
    v = str(settings().get("tape_format", "flac")).lower()
    return v if v in ("flac", "mp3") else "flac"


def opt_source() -> str:
    v = str(settings().get("tape_source", "player")).lower()
    return v if v in ("player", "output") else "player"


def opt_min_track() -> int:
    try:
        n = int(settings().get("tape_min_seconds", DEFAULT_MIN_TRACK_SEC))
    except Exception:
        return DEFAULT_MIN_TRACK_SEC
    return max(5, min(600, n))


def state() -> dict:
    return read_json(STATE)


def _cmdline(pid: int) -> bytes:
    try:
        return Path("/proc/%d/cmdline" % pid).read_bytes()
    except Exception:
        return b""


def pid_alive(pid: int) -> bool:
    if not pid or pid == os.getpid():
        return False
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
    except Exception:
        return False
    # ⚠ A recycled pid would read as alive. Confirm it is actually us before
    # believing the state file — a stale `armed: true` with somebody else's
    # pid would make the toggle un-pressable.
    return b"nyxus-tape" in _cmdline(pid)


def daemon_pids() -> list[int]:
    """EVERY tape daemon this user is running, found in /proc — never from
    the state file.                                              TRK-3829

    ⚠ THIS IS THE FIX FOR THE BUG THAT MADE THE STATE UNTRUSTWORTHY, and
    the shape of it is worth reading before touching anything here.

    `--disarm` used to SIGTERM the ONE pid the state file recorded, wait
    three seconds, and then write `armed: false` whether or not the daemon
    had actually gone. The daemon's SIGTERM handler sets a flag, but the
    main loop is blocked in `self.follow.stdout.readline()` — and under
    PEP 475 Python RETRIES an interrupted read, so the handler runs and the
    loop goes straight back to sleep until the player publishes another
    line. On a paused player that is never. So:

        press disarm → daemon survives → file says disarmed
        press arm    → arm() believes the file → A SECOND DAEMON

    Two daemons then recorded the same audio and took turns writing the one
    state file, which is precisely why "is it on?" could not be answered.
    Found on the owner's machine 2026-09-07 (two live pids, 25 MB of orphan
    PCM in his tmpfs). Three things fix it and all three are needed:
      • the daemon tears its children down inside the handler, so the
        readline returns EOF at once (Deck.run § bye);
      • stop_daemons() escalates to SIGKILL and CONFIRMS death;
      • arm() and disarm() both work from THIS list, not from the file, so
        a daemon the file has forgotten is still found and still stopped.

    Cheap: /proc scan of numeric entries only, no subprocess.
    """
    me = os.getpid()
    out = []
    try:
        names = os.listdir("/proc")
    except Exception:
        return out
    for name in names:
        if not name.isdigit():
            continue
        pid = int(name)
        if pid == me:
            continue
        cmd = _cmdline(pid)
        # ⚠ ARGV ELEMENTS, NOT A SUBSTRING OF THE WHOLE COMMAND LINE.
        #                                                     TRK-3959
        # This used to be `b"nyxus-tape" in cmd and b"--daemon" in cmd`, a
        # substring test against the raw NUL-joined buffer, so ANY process
        # whose command line merely MENTIONS both strings counted as a tape
        # daemon. Tripped by accident on 2026-09-08: one ssh command
        # containing the words `nyxus-tape --daemon` made `--status` report
        # `"daemons": 1` on a machine with no daemon running at all.
        #
        # ⚠ AND IT IS NOT A COSMETIC MISCOUNT, WHICH IS WHY IT IS FIXED
        # RATHER THAN NOTED. `arm()` and `disarm()` both work from this
        # list BY DESIGN (that is TRK-3829's fix and it is right), and
        # `stop_daemons()` escalates to SIGKILL and confirms death. So a
        # false positive here does not just miscount — it makes
        # `nyxus-tape --arm` SIGKILL an unrelated process because its
        # command line quoted this program's own name. A shell wrapper, a
        # log tail, `pgrep -af`, or an agent's own ssh line all qualify.
        # `--resume` reads the same list, so a stray match would also make
        # it silently decline to restore the tape.
        #
        # ⚠ AND ARGV MEMBERSHIP ALONE IS STILL NOT ENOUGH — round 1 of this
        # fix asserted exactly that and its own test passed for the wrong
        # reason (the decoy process died on startup, so nothing was ever
        # matched against). `sh -c 'sleep 5' nyxus-tape --daemon` has both
        # strings as genuine argv ELEMENTS and is not a tape.
        #
        # So: POSITION, not membership. The daemon is spawned exactly one
        # way — `arm()` runs `[sys.executable, exe, "--daemon"]` — and a
        # shebang launch is `[exe, "--daemon"]`. In both, this program is
        # argv[0] or argv[1] and `--daemon` is the argument right after it.
        # Nothing that merely quotes the name can occupy that slot.
        argv = [a for a in cmd.split(b"\0") if a]
        for i in (0, 1):
            if len(argv) > i + 1 \
                    and (argv[i] == b"nyxus-tape"
                         or argv[i].endswith(b"/nyxus-tape")) \
                    and argv[i + 1] == b"--daemon":
                out.append(pid)
                break
    return sorted(out)


def stop_daemons(pids: list[int] | None = None) -> int:
    """SIGTERM, confirm, SIGKILL, confirm. Returns how many are STILL alive.

    ⚠ Never reports success it has not measured: the caller writes the
    disarmed state only after this has come back 0.
    """
    live = [p for p in (daemon_pids() if pids is None else pids) if p]
    for pid in live:
        try:
            os.kill(pid, signal.SIGTERM)
        except Exception:
            pass
    deadline = time.time() + STOP_GRACE_SEC
    while time.time() < deadline:
        live = [p for p in live if pid_alive(p)]
        if not live:
            return 0
        time.sleep(0.05)
    for pid in live:
        try:
            os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        except Exception:
            pass
    deadline = time.time() + 2.0
    while time.time() < deadline:
        live = [p for p in live if pid_alive(p)]
        if not live:
            return 0
        time.sleep(0.05)
    return len(live)


def clean_scratch() -> int:
    """Delete the raw PCM scratch. Returns the bytes reclaimed.  TRK-3829

    ⚠ `/run/user/<uid>` IS RAM. A daemon killed with SIGKILL never runs
    its `finally`, so its half-written tape stays in tmpfs until the next
    reboot — 25 MB of the owner's memory was sitting there on 2026-09-07,
    from daemons that had already been killed. Called on every arm, on
    every disarm, and by the daemon before it starts recording.

    ⚠ CALLED ONLY WHEN NO DAEMON IS ALIVE. It would otherwise unlink the
    file a running tape is still writing into, and the song would be lost
    at the exact moment it was about to be kept.
    """
    freed = 0
    if daemon_pids():
        return 0
    d = runtime_dir()
    try:
        for f in d.iterdir():
            if f.suffix not in (".pcm", ".art"):
                continue
            try:
                freed += f.stat().st_size
                f.unlink()
            except Exception:
                pass
    except Exception:
        pass
    return freed


def live_state() -> dict:
    """The state file, corrected against reality. The daemon can be killed
    (a reboot, an OOM, a crash) without getting to rewrite the file, and a
    surface that believed it would show ARMED for ever.

    ⚠ `stale` is ALWAYS present now, true or false. It was documented in
    the status contract and only ever written on the failing branch, so a
    reader could not tell "fresh" from "this build does not say".

    ⚠ `daemons` is the /proc count, not a wish: two of them is the defect
    this file spent a night on and a surface should be able to see it.
    """
    s = state()
    pids = daemon_pids()
    recorded = int(s.get("pid") or 0)
    # Alive means: the recorded pid is one of the daemons actually running.
    # A daemon running under a pid the file has FORGOTTEN is not "armed" as
    # far as the file goes -- but it is still counted, and arm() stops it.
    alive = recorded in pids if recorded else False
    if s.get("armed") and not alive:
        s["armed"] = False
        s["taping"] = ""
    beat = int(s.get("beat") or 0)
    if s.get("armed") and beat and (time.time() - beat) > STALE_BEAT_SEC:
        # The pid is alive but the daemon has stopped stamping. Something is
        # wedged; do not tell a surface it is recording.
        s["armed"] = False
        s["taping"] = ""
    s["stale"] = bool(state().get("armed")) and not s.get("armed")
    s["daemons"] = len(pids)
    return s


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  which monitor
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

def run(cmd: list[str], timeout: float = 5.0) -> str:
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=timeout)
        return p.stdout
    except Exception:
        return ""


def default_sink() -> str:
    return run(["pactl", "get-default-sink"]).strip()


def player_sink() -> str:
    """The sink the player is actually feeding.

    ⚠ Parsed out of `pactl list sink-inputs` TEXT rather than `-f json`,
    because the json formatter is a newer pactl and this has to work on
    whatever the image ships. Text output is stable and has been for years.
    """
    out = run(["pactl", "list", "sink-inputs"])
    sink_no = ""
    for block in out.split("Sink Input #"):
        if not block.strip():
            continue
        m = re.search(r"^\s*Sink:\s*(\d+)\s*$", block, re.M)
        if not m:
            continue
        # any browser/player stream will do; the point is to find the sink
        # a *media* stream is on, not to identify the app
        if re.search(r'application\.name\s*=\s*"[^"]*"', block):
            sink_no = m.group(1)
            if re.search(r"media\.role\s*=\s*\"?music", block) or \
               re.search(r"media\.name\s*=\s*\"Playback\"", block):
                break
    if not sink_no:
        return ""
    for line in run(["pactl", "list", "short", "sinks"]).splitlines():
        parts = line.split("\t")
        if len(parts) >= 2 and parts[0].strip() == sink_no:
            return parts[1].strip()
    return ""


def monitor_source() -> str:
    """Resolve to a `.monitor` name. Falls back down a chain rather than
    failing, because 'no source' means the whole feature silently does
    nothing and the owner is never told why."""
    if opt_source() == "player":
        s = player_sink()
        if s:
            return s + ".monitor"
    s = default_sink()
    if s:
        return s + ".monitor"
    for line in run(["pactl", "list", "short", "sources"]).splitlines():
        parts = line.split("\t")
        if len(parts) >= 2 and parts[1].endswith(".monitor"):
            return parts[1]
    return ""


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  identity — the triple, NEVER the trackid
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

SEP = "\x1f"     # a unit separator: cannot occur in a title, unlike "|"
FOLLOW_FMT = ("{{status}}" + SEP + "{{xesam:artist}}" + SEP +
              "{{xesam:title}}" + SEP + "{{xesam:album}}" + SEP +
              "{{mpris:length}}" + SEP + "{{mpris:artUrl}}")


class Now:
    """One MPRIS observation."""

    __slots__ = ("status", "artist", "title", "album", "length", "art")

    def __init__(self, line: str = "") -> None:
        f = line.split(SEP)
        f += [""] * (6 - len(f))
        self.status = f[0].strip()
        self.artist = f[1].strip()
        self.title = f[2].strip()
        self.album = f[3].strip()
        try:
            self.length = int(f[4] or 0) / 1_000_000.0   # µs → s
        except Exception:
            self.length = 0.0
        self.art = f[5].strip()

    @property
    def ident(self) -> str:
        """The drift-proof half of identity: artist + title.

        ⚠ NOT `mpris:trackid` — it is one constant for the whole Chromium tab
        (see the module docstring), and the 01:44 ad break re-confirmed it:
        two adverts and two songs all carried Track5279E7E6D748D91A0EC5A4AF0…

        ⚠ AND NO LONGER `mpris:length` EITHER. MEASURED on the owner's
        machine 2026-09-07, mid-track and unprompted, nothing touched:

            01:45:41  Playing | Imperium Music Group | Pretty Problems | 131000000
            01:46:29  Playing | Imperium Music Group | Pretty Problems | 130901000

        **The player re-publishes `mpris:length` with a DIFFERENT value while
        the same track keeps playing** — 99 ms apart here. This used to key on
        `round(length)`, and 131.000 and 130.901 both round to 131, so that
        song survived on one millisecond of margin. Had the drift crossed a
        rounding boundary, `now.ident != t.ident` would have been true with
        `now.eligible()` also true — which `Deck.on` reads as A SKIP, and the
        response to a skip is to DELETE the tape. **A drifting field would
        have silently discarded finished songs, occasionally, with nothing
        logged and nothing to see.** Length is still a discriminator; it goes
        through `same_track()` with slack instead of through string equality.
        """
        return "%s%s%s" % (self.artist, SEP, self.title)

    def same_track(self, ident: str, length: float) -> bool:
        """Is this the same track a tape was opened on?

        Length still separates a radio edit from an album cut, but with
        LENGTH_TOL_SEC of slack because the player is not precise about it.
        """
        return (self.ident == ident
                and abs(self.length - length) <= LENGTH_TOL_SEC)

    @property
    def playing(self) -> bool:
        return self.status == "Playing"

    def eligible(self, floor: int) -> bool:
        """Is this something we are willing to OPEN A TAPE on?

        Not "is it a song" — nothing published here can answer that. This is
        the advert floor plus the requirement that it is actually named.
        """
        return (self.playing and bool(self.artist) and bool(self.title)
                and floor <= self.length <= MAX_TRACK_SEC
                # audit 2026-09-08: the floor alone kept 90-170 s trailers
                # and pharma spots as songs. Word list, owner-editable.
                and not looks_like_advert(self.artist, self.title))


_ADWORDS = HOME / ".config" / "nyxus" / "tape-adwords.txt"
_adwords_cache: tuple[float, list] = (0.0, [])



# ── audit 2026-09-08: no duplicates ───────────────────────────────────────
# `unique()` still guards the filesystem, but the owner's rule is that a song
# already in the library is not kept again. Same song = same lead artist +
# same title with the YouTube suffixes stripped + length within 4 % / 6 s.
_KEY_JUNK = re.compile(r"\s*[\(\[][^\)\]]*[\)\]]|\s+(?:feat|ft)\.?\s.*$|"
                       r"\s+-\s+(?:extended|remix|live|remaster(?:ed)?).*$", re.I)
_KEY_SPLIT = re.compile(r"\s*(?:&|,|;|/|\bx\b|\bfeat\.?\b|\bft\.?\b|\bfeaturing\b|\bwith\b|\band\b)\s+", re.I)
_kept_cache: tuple[float, dict] = (0.0, {})


def _norm_key(s: str) -> str:
    return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", " ", (s or "").lower()).strip()


def song_key(artist: str, title: str) -> str:
    lead = _KEY_SPLIT.split(artist or "", maxsplit=1)[0]
    t = _KEY_JUNK.sub("", title or "")
    if lead and t.lower().startswith(lead.lower()):
        t = re.sub(r"^\s*[-–—:|]\s*", "", t[len(lead):])
    return "%s|%s" % (_norm_key(lead), _norm_key(t.strip('"\'“”‘’ ')))


def kept_songs() -> dict:
    """{song_key: [durations]} from the keeps index, re-read when it changes."""
    global _kept_cache
    try:
        m = INDEX.stat().st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return {}
    if m != _kept_cache[0]:
        table: dict = {}
        try:
            for t in (read_json(INDEX) or {}).get("tracks", []):
                if not Path(t.get("path", "")).exists():
                    continue
                table.setdefault(song_key(t.get("artist", ""), t.get("title", "")), []) \
                     .append(float(t.get("duration") or 0))
        except Exception:
            table = {}
        _kept_cache = (m, table)
    return _kept_cache[1]


def already_kept(now) -> bool:
    durs = kept_songs().get(song_key(now.artist, now.title))
    if not durs:
        return False
    L = float(now.length or 0)
    return any(abs(d - L) <= max(6.0, 0.04 * L) for d in durs) if L else True


def looks_like_advert(artist: str, title: str) -> bool:
    """True if artist or title matches a line of ~/.config/nyxus/tape-adwords.txt.
    Re-read when the file changes, so a word the owner adds counts for the
    very next track. Never raises: a bad regex line is skipped."""
    global _adwords_cache
    try:
        m = _ADWORDS.stat().st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return False
    if m != _adwords_cache[0]:
        pats = []
        try:
            for line in _ADWORDS.read_text().splitlines():
                line = line.strip()
                if not line or line.startswith("#"):
                    continue
                try:
                    pats.append(re.compile(line, re.I))
                except re.error:
                    continue
        except OSError:
            pass
        _adwords_cache = (m, pats)
    hay = "%s\n%s" % (artist or "", title or "")
    return any(p.search(hay) for p in _adwords_cache[1])


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  naming
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

_BAD = re.compile(r'[/\\\x00-\x1f]')


def safe(part: str, fallback: str) -> str:
    """A filename component. Slashes and control characters only; everything
    else a person typed into a song title is allowed to survive, because a
    library that mangles titles is a library you stop trusting."""
    s = unicodedata.normalize("NFC", (part or "").strip())
    s = _BAD.sub("-", s).strip(" .")
    return s[:120] or fallback


def track_paths(now: Now, fmt: str) -> tuple[Path, Path]:
    """<keeps>/<Artist>/<Album>/<Title>.<ext>

    ⚠ 'Unknown artist' / 'Unknown album' are DELIBERATE, and they are the
    honest answer. MPRIS genuinely does not always publish an album — a track
    played from a YouTube video has none — and filing it under a plausible
    guess would put it somewhere the owner cannot find it and cannot correct.
    The Media app already sorts empties last for exactly this reason.
    """
    artist = safe(now.artist, "Unknown artist")
    album = safe(now.album, "Unknown album")
    title = safe(now.title, "Untitled")
    folder = KEEPS_DIR / artist / album
    ext = "flac" if fmt == "flac" else "mp3"
    return folder / ("%s.%s" % (title, ext)), folder / ("%s.lrc" % title)


def unique(path: Path) -> Path:
    """Never overwrite. A second capture of the same song is a second file,
    not a silent replacement of one the owner may already have curated."""
    if not path.exists():
        return path
    stem, suf = path.stem, path.suffix
    for n in range(2, 100):
        cand = path.with_name("%s (%d)%s" % (stem, n, suf))
        if not cand.exists():
            return cand
    return path.with_name("%s (%d)%s" % (stem, int(time.time()), suf))


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  lyrics — REUSE the fetcher this build already ships
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

def lrc_from_cache(now: Now, dest: Path) -> bool:
    """Write a STANDARD `[mm:ss.xx]` .lrc beside the audio.

    ⚠ The words are NOT fetched a second way. `/usr/local/bin/nyxus-lyrics`
    already does the lookup, already caches it, and already carries the
    owner's `lyrics_online` consent — it checks that key itself before it
    opens a socket, so calling it when consent is off is a no-op, not a leak.
    We ask it for the path and read the file it names.

    ⚠ Lines are rebuilt from the `anchor` flag, which is the fifth field and
    means "this word's start is a LITERAL timestamp off the source LRC — the
    first word of a line". Grouping on it reconstructs the original lines
    exactly. A schema-1 document has only four fields and no anchor, so no
    .lrc is written rather than one line per word, which no player wants.
    """
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            ["nyxus-lyrics", "--artist", now.artist, "--title", now.title,
             "--duration", str(int(round(now.length)))],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30).stdout.strip()
        if not out.startswith("/"):
            return False
        doc = read_json(Path(out))
        words = doc.get("words") or []
        if doc.get("miss") or not words or len(words[0]) < 5:
            return False
        lines: list[tuple[float, list[str]]] = []
        for w in words:
            start, text, anchor = float(w[0]), str(w[2]), bool(w[4])
            if anchor or not lines:
                lines.append((start, [text]))
            else:
                lines[-1][1].append(text)
        body = ["[re:nyxus-tape]", "[ti:%s]" % now.title,
                "[ar:%s]" % now.artist]
        if now.album:
            body.append("[al:%s]" % now.album)
        for start, ws in lines:
            m, s = divmod(max(0.0, start), 60)
            body.append("[%02d:%05.2f]%s" % (int(m), s, " ".join(ws)))
        dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        dest.write_text("\n".join(body) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
        return True
    except Exception:
        return False


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  the cover ledger                                                 TRK-3859
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

def album_key(artist: str, album: str) -> str:
    """Which ALBUM an image was seen under. Normalised the same way
    `safe()` is not -- this is an identity, not a filename.

    ── ⚠ THE ARTIST IS NOT PART OF THIS KEY, AND IT USED TO BE ───────────
    TRK-3960, and it convicted four real album covers on his own machine.

    The rule this ledger enforces is "an image that turns up under more than
    one ALBUM is not a cover". The key it actually compared was
    `artist \\x1f album`, so ANY of these made one album look like two:

      * a featured credit -- `xesam:artist` is "Nicki Minaj" on one track of
        Beam Me Up Scotty and "Nicki Minaj & Drake" on the next;
      * a compilation credit -- "Cardi B" vs
        "Cardi B, Bad Bunny, & J Balvin" across Invasion of Privacy;
      * an edition suffix -- "The Pinkprint (Deluxe)" and "The Pinkprint
        (Deluxe Edition)" are one record with two spellings.

    MEASURED in `~/.local/share/nyxus/media/cover-ledger.json` at
    2026-09-08 04:20, against the file mtimes in ~/Music/NYXUS Keeps. Each
    conviction's `seen` stamp equals the mtime of the song being kept at
    that moment TO THE MINUTE, which is what makes this causal rather than
    circumstantial -- the cover was refused as the file was written:

      09-07 19:42  0e7f..→121a3e7575  Pink Friday: Roman Reloaded The Re-Up
      09-07 20:10  44624c2447          Beam Me Up Scotty
      09-07 21:22  0e7fe53206          The Pinkprint (Deluxe)
      09-07 22:51  66dd686374          Invasion of Privacy

    FOUR of the ten art-less songs in his library are these. The other six
    are correct: four are the Chromium logo being properly refused, and two
    (Planet Rock (Bonus Beats), Put You In A Room) were published with no
    picture at all.

    ⚠ SO: album only, with bracketed qualifiers stripped. Two records that
    genuinely share a title now share a key, which can only ever make the
    guard MORE cautious -- it merges albums, so it convicts less. That is
    the safe direction for a rule whose failure mode is destroying a real
    cover, and `COVER_ICON_ALBUMS` below is the other half of the answer.
    """
    s = unicodedata.normalize("NFKD", (album or "").strip())
    s = "".join(c for c in s if not unicodedata.combining(c))
    s = s.casefold()
    # "(Deluxe)", "(Deluxe Edition)", "[Explicit]", "(Remastered 2011)" --
    # editions of one record, not different records.
    s = re.sub(r"\s*[\(\[][^\)\]]*[\)\]]\s*", " ", s)
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", s).strip()


def image_digest(path: Path) -> str:
    """sha1 of the image's own bytes, or "" if it cannot be read."""
    try:
        h = hashlib.sha1()
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            for block in iter(lambda: fh.read(65536), b""):
                h.update(block)
        return h.hexdigest()
    except Exception:
        return ""


def cover_albums(row: dict) -> list[str] | None:
    """The album keys a ledger row has seen, or None for a row this build
    cannot read.

    ⚠ A PRE-TRK-3960 ROW IS "NO OPINION", NEVER A VERDICT. Those rows were
    written under the artist-bearing key and their `icon` flag was computed
    by the rule that convicted four of his real covers. Honouring that flag
    would carry the defect forward for ever, so a `v1` row is ignored and
    `--covers-scan` rebuilds the ledger from the files, which are and always
    were the authority.
    """
    if not isinstance(row, dict):
        return None
    albums = row.get("albums")
    return albums if isinstance(albums, list) else None


def cover_verdict(digest: str, key: str) -> bool:
    """True if this image may be embedded as THIS track's cover.

    ⚠ THE LEDGER IS ADVISORY AND MUST NEVER COST THE SONG. Every failure
    path here returns True: an unreadable, corrupt, absent or older-schema
    ledger means the tape behaves exactly as it did before this existed.

    ⚠ THE VERDICT IS DERIVED, NOT STORED.               TRK-3960
    It used to be read out of a sticky `icon` flag -- "already convicted;
    stays convicted" -- which meant a conviction made under a rule that was
    later found wrong could never be undone, and four of them were wrong.
    The flag is still WRITTEN, for `--covers-scan`'s report and for anything
    that reads the file, but it is recomputed from `albums` every time it is
    written and nothing reads it back as authority. This repo's standing
    ruling: derive it, do not enumerate it.

    Convictions are still monotonic in practice -- `albums` only grows -- so
    the guarantee the sticky flag was protecting is unchanged.
    """
    if not digest:
        return True
    doc = read_json(COVERS)
    row = (doc.get("images") or {}).get(digest)
    albums = cover_albums(row)
    if albums is None:
        return True
    seen = set(albums)
    seen.add(key)
    return len(seen) < COVER_ICON_ALBUMS


def cover_record(digest: str, key: str, embedded: bool) -> None:
    """Remember what this image was seen under, and convict it once it has
    turned up under `COVER_ICON_ALBUMS` genuinely different albums."""
    if not digest:
        return
    try:
        doc = read_json(COVERS)
        images = doc.get("images")
        if not isinstance(images, dict):
            images = {}
        row = images.get(digest)
        if not isinstance(row, dict):
            row = {}
        # A pre-TRK-3960 row starts its history empty rather than carrying
        # its old artist-bearing key forward: that string is in a different
        # key space and would sit in the set for ever, one permanent phantom
        # album pushing every image one step closer to conviction.
        albums = cover_albums(row) or []
        if key not in albums:
            albums.append(key)
        row["albums"] = albums[-COVER_ALBUMS_KEPT:]
        row["album"] = key
        row["n"] = int(row.get("n") or 0) + 1
        row["seen"] = int(time.time())
        row["embedded"] = bool(embedded)
        row["icon"] = len(set(row["albums"])) >= COVER_ICON_ALBUMS
        images[digest] = row
        if len(images) > COVER_LEDGER_MAX:
            oldest = sorted(images.items(),
                            key=lambda kv: kv[1].get("seen", 0))
            for d, _r in oldest[:len(images) - COVER_LEDGER_MAX]:
                images.pop(d, None)
        write_json(COVERS, {"v": 1, "images": images})
    except Exception:
        pass


def covers_scan() -> int:
    """Seed the ledger from the songs already kept.  `nyxus-tape --covers-scan`

    ⚠ THIS RETROFITS NOTHING. It opens no file for writing, re-tags nothing
    and deletes nothing; it reads the embedded picture out of each kept
    track, hashes it, and records which album it was found under. The 92
    songs already saved keep exactly the art they have. What changes is that
    the NEXT capture can be judged against real history instead of against
    an empty ledger.

    ⚠ AND IT IS ONLY AS GOOD AS THE BYTES MATCHING. The tape hashes the file
    `artUrl` pointed at; this hashes what ffmpeg copied into the container.
    `-c:v copy` is a byte copy in both directions, so they agree -- but if a
    future encoder ever transcodes the picture the digests diverge and this
    seeding simply stops helping. It cannot make anything worse: a digest
    that matches nothing is a first sighting, which is already the
    unjudgeable case.
    """
    if not KEEPS_DIR.is_dir():
        print("no keeps directory at %s" % KEEPS_DIR)
        return 0
    seen = 0
    files = sorted(f for f in KEEPS_DIR.rglob("*")
                   if f.suffix.lower() in (".flac", ".mp3"))
    # ⚠ REBUILT, NOT ACCUMULATED ONTO.                        TRK-3960
    # This used to call `cover_record` per file, which merges into whatever
    # the ledger already held. That is wrong for a scan whose whole premise
    # is that THE FILES ARE THE AUTHORITY, and it is doubly wrong across the
    # key change: a pre-TRK-3960 ledger holds `artist \x1f album` strings
    # that no longer resolve to anything, and merging would leave every
    # image carrying one dead album key for ever. A fresh dict built from
    # 315 real files on disk needs no migration rules at all.
    images: dict[str, dict] = {}
    now = int(time.time())
    for f in files:
        try:
            pic = subprocess.run(
                ["ffmpeg", "-hide_banner", "-loglevel", "error", "-i", str(f),
                 "-an", "-c:v", "copy", "-f", "image2pipe", "-"],
                capture_output=True, timeout=60)
            if pic.returncode != 0 or not pic.stdout:
                continue
            digest = hashlib.sha1(pic.stdout).hexdigest()
        except Exception:
            continue
        # <keeps>/<Artist>/<Album>/<Title>.<ext>
        key = album_key(f.parent.parent.name, f.parent.name)
        row = images.setdefault(digest, {"albums": [], "n": 0,
                                         "seen": now, "embedded": True})
        if key not in row["albums"]:
            row["albums"].append(key)
        row["n"] += 1
    for row in images.values():
        row["albums"] = row["albums"][:COVER_ALBUMS_KEPT]
        row["album"] = row["albums"][-1] if row["albums"] else ""
        row["icon"] = len(set(row["albums"])) >= COVER_ICON_ALBUMS
    seen = sum(r["n"] for r in images.values())
    write_json(COVERS, {"v": 1, "images": images})
    icons = sum(1 for r in images.values() if r.get("icon"))
    print("scanned %d of %d kept files; %d distinct images; "
          "%d convicted as source icons (>= %d albums)"
          % (seen, len(files), len(images), icons, COVER_ICON_ALBUMS))
    return 0


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  the tape
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

class Tape:
    """One track's scratch recording, gated on its own identity."""

    def __init__(self, now: Now, scratch: Path) -> None:
        self.ident = now.ident
        self.now = now
        self.path = scratch / ("tape-%d.pcm" % int(time.time() * 1000))
        self.fh = open(self.path, "wb")
        self.written = 0
        self.open_gate = True
        self.held_since = 0.0
        self.seams = 0
        self.art: Path | None = None
        self.art_url = ""
        self.opened = time.time()
        # ⚠ COPY THE COVER NOW. `mpris:artUrl` points at a Chromium temp file
        # (/tmp/.org.chromium.Chromium.XXXXXX) that is replaced or unlinked
        # at the next track change. Reading it at finalise time reads either
        # the WRONG song's cover or nothing at all.
        self.grab_art(now)

    def grab_art(self, now: Now) -> None:
        """Copy the bytes `artUrl` currently names, if they are new."""
        url = (now.art or "").strip()
        if not url.startswith("file://") or url == self.art_url:
            return
        src = Path(url[7:])
        try:
            if not src.is_file():
                return
            dst = self.path.with_suffix(".art")
            shutil.copyfile(src, dst)
            self.art = dst
            self.art_url = url
        except Exception:
            pass

    def settle_art(self, now: Now) -> None:
        """Take a LATER artUrl for the same track, for a little while.

        ⚠ THIS IS THE FIX, AND IT IS ABOUT TIMING, NOT ABOUT WHERE THE ART
        COMES FROM.                                              TRK-3859
        A tape opens at the moment the metadata changed, which is the
        earliest and therefore worst moment to read `artUrl`: the player
        publishes a track's fields in stages and the picture is routinely
        the LAST thing to resolve. Every line `playerctl --follow` emits for
        a track this deck is already recording is another, later look at the
        same question, and the last answer inside the settle window is the
        one that gets embedded.

        ⚠ THE WINDOW IS BOUNDED ON PURPOSE. Past ART_SETTLE_SEC an artUrl
        change is far more likely to be the NEXT track arriving early than
        this one resolving late, and taking it would reintroduce exactly the
        wrong-cover defect from the other end.
        """
        if time.time() - self.opened <= ART_SETTLE_SEC:
            self.grab_art(now)

    @property
    def seconds(self) -> float:
        return self.written / float(BYTES_PER_SEC)

    def feed(self, data: bytes) -> None:
        if self.open_gate:
            try:
                self.fh.write(data)
                self.written += len(data)
            except Exception:
                pass

    def gate(self, opened: bool) -> None:
        if opened == self.open_gate:
            return
        self.open_gate = opened
        if opened:
            self.seams += 1
            self.held_since = 0.0
        else:
            self.held_since = time.time()

    def complete(self) -> bool:
        return self.seconds >= COMPLETE_FRACTION * self.now.length

    def close(self) -> None:
        try:
            self.fh.close()
        except Exception:
            pass

    def discard(self) -> None:
        self.close()
        for p in (self.path, self.art):
            try:
                if p:
                    p.unlink()
            except Exception:
                pass


def encode(tape: Tape, fmt: str, source: str = "") -> Path | None:
    """Trim, encode, tag and embed the cover in ONE ffmpeg invocation.

    ⚠ Trimmed FROM THE FRONT: the end boundary is the reliable one, so we
    keep the last `length` seconds. That discards the pre-roll and any tail
    of the previous track without having to know the sink's latency.
    """
    dest, lrc = track_paths(tape.now, fmt)
    dest = unique(dest)
    dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

    skip = max(0.0, tape.seconds - tape.now.length)
    cmd = ["ffmpeg", "-hide_banner", "-loglevel", "error", "-y",
           "-f", "s16le", "-ar", str(RATE), "-ac", str(CHANNELS)]
    if skip > 0.001:
        cmd += ["-ss", "%.3f" % skip]
    cmd += ["-i", str(tape.path)]
    # ⚠ IS THIS ACTUALLY A COVER? See the COVER LEDGER block above: an
    # image that has already been seen under a different album is a player
    # icon or a channel avatar, and embedding it is worse than embedding
    # nothing. The digest is recorded either way, so the SECOND sighting of
    # a source icon convicts it even though the first could not be judged.
    have_art = tape.art is not None and tape.art.exists()
    art_digest = image_digest(tape.art) if have_art else ""
    art_key = album_key(tape.now.artist, tape.now.album)
    if have_art and not cover_verdict(art_digest, art_key):
        have_art = False
    cover_record(art_digest, art_key, have_art)
    if have_art:
        cmd += ["-i", str(tape.art)]

    if fmt == "mp3":
        cmd += ["-c:a", "libmp3lame", "-b:a", "320k", "-id3v2_version", "3"]
    else:
        cmd += ["-c:a", "flac", "-compression_level", "5"]

    cmd += ["-map", "0:a"]
    if have_art:
        cmd += ["-map", "1:v", "-c:v", "copy", "-disposition:v",
                "attached_pic", "-metadata:s:v", "title=Album cover"]
    for k, v in (("title", tape.now.title), ("artist", tape.now.artist),
                 ("album", tape.now.album or "Unknown album"),
                 ("comment", "Kept by NYXUS from this machine's own audio "
                             "output, in real time.")):
        if v:
            cmd += ["-metadata", "%s=%s" % (k, v)]
    cmd += [str(dest)]

    try:
        p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=300)
        if p.returncode != 0 or not dest.exists() or dest.stat().st_size < 4096:
            try:
                dest.unlink()
            except Exception:
                pass
            return None
    except Exception:
        return None

    words = lrc.with_name(dest.stem + ".lrc")
    got_words = lrc_from_cache(tape.now, words)
    index_add(dest, words if got_words else None, tape.now, fmt, source,
              have_art)
    # audit 2026-09-08: Chromium's MPRIS art is 120x120. Upgrade the cover
    # (and an "Unknown album" tag) from the iTunes Search API, detached, so
    # the keep itself never waits on the network. Best effort.
    try:
        upgrader = shutil.which("nyxus-keeps-art")
        if upgrader:
            subprocess.Popen([upgrader, "--file", str(dest)],
                             stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                             stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True)
    except Exception:
        pass
    return dest


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  the daemon
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

class Deck:
    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.scratch = runtime_dir()
        self.scratch.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        self.fmt = opt_format()
        self.floor = opt_min_track()
        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        # ⚠ Serialises the follow thread's `on()` against the shutdown
        # path's `close_tape()`. Without it a SIGTERM landing mid-boundary
        # could encode and discard the same tape from two threads. TRK-3829
        self.deck = threading.RLock()
        self.tape: Tape | None = None
        self.preroll = bytearray()
        self.preroll_cap = int(PREROLL_SEC * BYTES_PER_SEC)
        self.kept = 0
        self.last = ""
        self.stop = threading.Event()
        self.source = monitor_source()
        self.parec: subprocess.Popen | None = None
        self.follow: subprocess.Popen | None = None
        # ⚠ ARMING MID-SONG MUST NOT TAPE THAT SONG. `playerctl --follow`
        # emits the CURRENT track immediately on connect, so without this the
        # very first line looks exactly like a track boundary and opens a tape
        # on a song that is already half over. Caught on the owner's machine
        # 2026-09-07: armed 115 s into a 209 s track and the deck reported
        # "taping" it. The completion floor would have thrown the fragment
        # away, so nothing wrong would have been SAVED -- but the state file
        # said "taping <title>", which is the surface lying to him about what
        # he is going to get. The first observation is a BASELINE, never a
        # boundary.
        self.primed = False

    # ── state file, the one thing both surfaces read ──────────────────────
    def publish(self, taping: str = None) -> None:
        with self.lock:
            t = self.tape
            label = taping if taping is not None else (
                ("%s — %s" % (t.now.artist, t.now.title)) if t else "")
        write_json(STATE, {
            "v": 1, "armed": True, "pid": os.getpid(),
            # ⚠ THE HEARTBEAT.                                    TRK-3829
            # The bar reads this FILE, not `--status`, so it has no pid to
            # check: a SIGKILLed daemon would leave `armed: true` lit on his
            # bar for ever, which is the same lie in a prettier place. The
            # daemon re-stamps `beat` every BEAT_SEC while it lives, and
            # TapeState.qml treats a heartbeat older than STALE_BEAT_SEC as
            # OFF. So the indicator decays to off by itself when the thing
            # it describes stops existing, with nothing to poll and no
            # process spawned on the bar.
            "beat": int(time.time()),
            "taping": label, "kept": self.kept, "last": self.last,
            "source": self.source, "format": self.fmt,
            "dir": str(KEEPS_DIR),
        })

    def heartbeat(self) -> None:
        """Re-stamp `beat` while nothing else is writing. A tape that plays
        one 4-minute song publishes twice; the surfaces need to know it is
        alive in between."""
        while not self.stop.wait(BEAT_SEC):
            try:
                self.publish()
            except Exception:
                pass

    # ── the audio thread: read forever, write only through the gate ───────
    def pump(self) -> None:
        assert self.parec and self.parec.stdout
        while not self.stop.is_set():
            data = self.parec.stdout.read(CHUNK)
            if not data:
                break
            with self.lock:
                t = self.tape
                if t is None:
                    # keep a short pre-roll so a track opened on the MPRIS
                    # boundary cannot lose its own first samples
                    self.preroll += data
                    if len(self.preroll) > self.preroll_cap:
                        del self.preroll[:-self.preroll_cap]
                else:
                    t.feed(data)

    # ── tape lifecycle ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    def open_tape(self, now: Now) -> None:
        with self.lock:
            t = Tape(now, self.scratch)
            t.feed(bytes(self.preroll))
            self.preroll.clear()
            self.tape = t
        self.publish()

    def close_tape(self, save: bool) -> None:
        with self.lock:
            t, self.tape = self.tape, None
        if t is None:
            return
        t.close()
        if not save or not t.complete():
            t.discard()
            self.publish("")
            return
        dest = encode(t, self.fmt, self.source)
        t.discard()
        if dest:
            self.kept += 1
            self.last = "%s — %s" % (t.now.artist, t.now.title)
        self.publish("")

    def on(self, now: Now) -> None:
        if not self.primed:
            # baseline only: whatever is playing at the moment of arming is
            # already past its start and can never be a whole track
            self.primed = True
            self.base = now.ident
            self.publish("")
            return
        if now.ident == getattr(self, "base", None) and self.tape is None:
            # still the song he armed during -- not a boundary, still skipped
            return
        t = self.tape
        if t is not None:
            if now.same_track(t.ident, t.now.length) and now.playing:
                t.gate(True)
                t.settle_art(now)            # TRK-3859 — a later, better look
                return
            # ⚠ deviation: an advert, a boundary, a blank, or a skip.
            t.gate(False)
            if t.complete():
                self.close_tape(True)               # natural end
            elif now.eligible(self.floor) and not now.same_track(t.ident,
                                                                t.now.length):
                # a different real track began: he skipped. Drop the fragment
                # — this is exactly what the button exists to prevent — and
                # start a clean tape on the new one.
                self.close_tape(False)
            elif t.held_since and time.time() - t.held_since > HOLD_SEC:
                self.close_tape(False)              # it never came back
            else:
                return                              # hold: advert in progress
            t = None
        if now.eligible(self.floor) and not already_kept(now):
            self.base = None
            self.open_tape(now)
        else:
            self.publish("")

    # ── the metadata thread: every track boundary arrives here ───────────
    def follow_loop(self, env: dict) -> None:
        """`playerctl --follow`, read on a thread of its own.    TRK-3829

        ⚠ It is a DAEMON thread on purpose: it can be blocked in a read that
        will never return (a paused player publishes nothing at all), and
        the process must still be able to exit the moment it is told to.
        `self.deck` serialises it against the shutdown path so a tape cannot
        be closed twice or half-encoded on the way out.
        """
        while not self.stop.is_set():
            f = self.follow
            if f is None or f.stdout is None:
                return
            line = f.stdout.readline()
            if self.stop.is_set():
                return
            if not line:
                # ⚠ the player vanished. That is 'not my track', not an
                # error: close the gate and wait for it to come back.
                with self.deck:
                    self.on(Now(""))
                if self.stop.wait(1.0):
                    return
                try:
                    self.follow = subprocess.Popen(
                        ["playerctl", "metadata", "--follow", "--format",
                         FOLLOW_FMT], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                        stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, env=env)
                except Exception:
                    return
                continue
            with self.deck:
                self.on(Now(line.rstrip("\n")))

    # ── run ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    def run(self) -> int:
        if not self.source:
            return 3
        # ⚠ Belt and braces with arm(): a daemon started by hand, or one
        # whose predecessor was SIGKILLed, must not inherit orphan PCM in
        # his RAM. No-ops if any other daemon is alive. TRK-3829
        clean_scratch()
        env = dict(os.environ)
        self.parec = subprocess.Popen(
            ["parec", "--device=" + self.source, "--format=s16le",
             "--rate=%d" % RATE, "--channels=%d" % CHANNELS,
             "--latency-msec=50"],
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, env=env)
        self.follow = subprocess.Popen(
            ["playerctl", "metadata", "--follow", "--format", FOLLOW_FMT],
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True,
            env=env)

        def bye(*_a):
            # ⚠ SETTING A FLAG THE MAIN LOOP CANNOT SEE IS THE WHOLE BUG.
            # TRK-3829. The follow reader blocks in `stdout.readline()`, and
            # PEP 475 makes Python RESUME that read once the handler returns
            # -- so the old daemon set its flag and went straight back to
            # sleep until the player published another line. On a PAUSED
            # player that never came: `--disarm` gave up, wrote
            # `armed: false`, and left the daemon recording; the next press
            # started a SECOND one. Two fixes, because either alone can be
            # defeated. (a) the read now lives in a DAEMON THREAD and the
            # main thread waits on the event, so nothing blocking can hold
            # the shutdown up; (b) the children are torn down here, so the
            # reader unblocks too instead of sitting on a dead pipe.
            self.stop.set()
            for p in (self.follow, self.parec):
                try:
                    if p:
                        p.terminate()
                except Exception:
                    pass
        signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, bye)
        signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, bye)

        threading.Thread(target=self.pump, daemon=True).start()
        threading.Thread(target=self.heartbeat, daemon=True).start()
        threading.Thread(target=self.follow_loop, args=(env,),
                         daemon=True).start()
        self.publish("")

        try:
            # ⚠ THE MAIN THREAD OWNS SHUTDOWN AND NOTHING ELSE. It holds no
            # pipe, no socket and no lock, so a SIGTERM is answered in one
            # wait interval no matter what the player, the sink or the
            # encoder is doing. Measured: 5.13 s (SIGKILL escalation) before
            # this change, 0.11 s after, against a silent player.
            while not self.stop.wait(0.1):
                pass
        finally:
            # ⚠ Disarming mid-track DELETES the tape. It never leaves a stub.
            with self.deck:
                self.close_tape(False)
            for p in (self.parec, self.follow):
                try:
                    if p:
                        p.terminate()
                        p.wait(timeout=2)
                except Exception:
                    try:
                        if p:
                            p.kill()
                    except Exception:
                        pass
            s = state()
            # ⚠ ONLY IF THE FILE IS STILL OURS. A daemon that lost a race
            # and is exiting after its replacement published must not
            # overwrite the live one's `armed: true` with its own `false` --
            # that is the two-daemon defect turned into a stuck OFF.
            if int(s.get("pid") or 0) in (0, os.getpid()):
                write_json(STATE, {"v": 1, "armed": False, "pid": 0,
                                   "beat": 0, "taping": "",
                                   "kept": self.kept, "last": self.last,
                                   "dir": str(KEEPS_DIR),
                                   "format": s.get("format", self.fmt)})
            clean_scratch()
        return 0


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  arm / disarm
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

def missing_tools() -> list[str]:
    return [t for t in ("parec", "playerctl", "ffmpeg")
            if shutil.which(t) is None]


def resume() -> int:
    """Put the tape back the way he left it, once, at session start.

    ── ⚠ THIS IS THE BUG THAT ATE HIS NIGHT, AND IT WAS WRITTEN DOWN ──────
                                                             TRK-3959
    `arm()`'s own docstring said it in as many words -- "NO SYSTEMD UNIT ...
    nothing survives a reboot" -- and nothing ever acted on it. MEASURED on
    his machine at 2026-09-08 04:05:

        ~/.config/nyxus/tape.json   armed: true, beat 1788838833
        beat 1788838833           = 2026-09-07 23:40:33
        newest file in NYXUS Keeps  2026-09-07 23:40
        `nyxus-tape --status`       "stale": true, "daemons": 0
        `who -b`                    system boot 2026-09-08 03:09

    So: he armed the tape, it kept songs until the session ended at 23:40,
    the machine rebooted at 03:09, AND THE TAPE NEVER CAME BACK. His report
    was "i had a song on before this one playing now so it didnt record it",
    and the answer is not that the song failed the 95 % gate or the advert
    floor -- THERE WAS NO DAEMON. Zero songs were kept between 23:40 and
    04:05 because nothing was listening.

    ⚠ AND THE FILE WAS LEFT SAYING `armed: true`, which is why this is a
    resume and not an autostart. The daemon is SIGKILLed by the session
    teardown and never gets to write `armed: false`, so the state on disk is
    his last INSTRUCTION, still standing. Honouring it is restoring what he
    asked for; ignoring it is silently countermanding him every boot.

    ⚠ IT READS THE RAW FILE, NOT `live_state()`. `live_state()` corrects
    `armed` against /proc, and at this moment the correction is exactly the
    thing being repaired -- the recorded pid is dead BY DEFINITION here, so
    the corrected view says "off" and a resume built on it could never fire.
    The raw flag is the instruction; /proc is only consulted to make sure we
    are not starting a second daemon.

    ⚠ IT NEVER ARMS A TAPE HE DID NOT ARM. `armed: false`, a missing file, a
    corrupt file -- all return 0 having started nothing. A fresh machine has
    no file and stays off, which is the shipped state.
    """
    raw = state()
    if raw.get("armed") is not True:
        return 0                      # he turned it off. Leave it off.
    if missing_tools():
        return 4
    if daemon_pids():
        # Something is already running -- a session that did not fully tear
        # down, or two logins. arm() is the one place allowed to reconcile
        # that, and it does; do not open a race with it here.
        return 0
    return arm()


def arm() -> int:
    """⚠ NO SYSTEMD UNIT, AND STILL NONE — the daemon is a plain detached
    child of the press that started it. What DID change on 2026-09-08 is
    that `--resume` (above) re-honours a standing `armed: true` once per
    session, so a reboot no longer silently countermands him. A boot with
    `armed: false` still begins, correctly, disarmed.

    ⚠ EXACTLY ONE DAEMON IS AN INVARIANT ENFORCED HERE, NOT AN ASSUMPTION.
    TRK-3829. This used to read the state file and return early if it said
    armed -- which is fine when the file is right, and is the second half of
    the two-daemon bug when it is not: a survived disarm leaves a running
    daemon with `armed: false` on disk, and this function then cheerfully
    started another. It now works from `daemon_pids()`, the /proc truth:
      • the recorded daemon is the only one running -> nothing to do;
      • anything else at all (an extra, an orphan the file forgot, a live
        daemon under a stale disarmed file) -> stop EVERY one of them and
        start a single clean daemon.
    """
    if missing_tools():
        return 4
    pids = daemon_pids()
    recorded = int(state().get("pid") or 0)
    if pids == [recorded] and live_state().get("armed"):
        return 0                                   # already armed, and true
    if pids:
        # An orphan, an extra, or a daemon the file has forgotten. Whichever
        # it is, the owner pressed ARM and must end up with one tape running
        # against the CURRENT settings -- not with somebody's leftover.
        stop_daemons(pids)
    clean_scratch()
    exe = os.path.realpath(__file__)
    subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, exe, "--daemon"],
                     stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                     stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True)
    for _ in range(40):
        time.sleep(0.05)
        if live_state().get("armed"):
            return 0
    return 5


def disarm() -> int:
    """Stop EVERY daemon, confirm it, then write the disarmed state.

    ⚠ THE ORDER IS THE FIX.                                    TRK-3829
    The old version SIGTERMed one pid, waited three seconds, and wrote
    `armed: false` unconditionally -- so a daemon that had not died left the
    file claiming a state that was not true, and every surface bound to that
    file was then lying. Now the file is written only after `stop_daemons()`
    has come back 0, and a survivor is reported as exit 6 with the state
    left ARMED, because a control that cannot stop the tape must not draw
    itself as off.

    ⚠ Works from `daemon_pids()`, never from the recorded pid alone: the pid
    in the file is exactly the thing that is wrong in the case that matters.
    """
    s = state()
    left = stop_daemons()
    if left:
        return 6
    write_json(STATE, {"v": 1, "armed": False, "pid": 0, "beat": 0,
                       "taping": "",
                       "kept": s.get("kept", 0), "last": s.get("last", ""),
                       "format": s.get("format", opt_format()),
                       "dir": str(KEEPS_DIR)})
    clean_scratch()
    return 0


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=True, description=__doc__)
    g = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=True)
    for flag in ("arm", "disarm", "toggle", "status", "probe", "daemon",
                 "covers-scan", "resume"):
        g.add_argument("--" + flag, action="store_true")
    a = ap.parse_args()

    if getattr(a, "covers_scan", False):
        return covers_scan()
    if a.resume:
        return resume()
    if a.probe:
        miss = missing_tools()
        print(json.dumps({"v": 1, "ok": not miss, "missing": miss,
                          "dir": str(KEEPS_DIR)}))
        return 0
    if a.status:
        s = live_state()
        s.setdefault("armed", False)
        s["dir"] = str(KEEPS_DIR)
        s["format"] = opt_format()
        s["ok"] = not missing_tools()
        print(json.dumps(s))
        return 0
    if a.daemon:
        return Deck().run()
    if a.arm:
        return arm()
    if a.disarm:
        return disarm()
    if a.toggle:
        return disarm() if live_state().get("armed") else arm()
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
    except Exception:
        # ⚠ FAILS INVISIBLY. Nothing here ever raises into the owner's
        # session; a broken tape is a tape that quietly did not happen.
        sys.exit(1)
